WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.889
Welcome to the town of Asbestos, Quebec.

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:06.270
These days, 'asbestos' is a word with
a sense of doom attached to it.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:11.020
But when this mine was opened in the
late 19th century, that really wasn't the case.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.670
Which is why they named the town after it.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:17.860
This pit just here was the biggest
asbestos mine in the world.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.320
Asbestos is a mineral,
easily mined in pits like this.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.619
It can be turned into strong, cheap,
fire-resistant insulation,

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:27.369
and it was used in a huge number
of building projects in the 20th century.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:32.880
Either as big insulation blocks, or in
ceiling tiles, or just sprayed on as cladding.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.370
It was a miracle substance:

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:39.450
it could even be woven into clothing, like
military uniforms or firefighters' gloves.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.440
The catch is that it's made up of
microscopic little fibrous crystals.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.720
If you break asbestos, drop it,
sandpaper it,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.730
turn it into insulation and spray it around,

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.750
those little fibres get into the air
and build up in people's lungs.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.390
Decades later, people who've worked with absestos,
or lived near an asbestos mine,

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.840
they tend to develop a particularly nasty
type of cancer called mesothelioma.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.080
Any exposure to asbestos fibres, however small,
can be dangerous.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.359
And in this town, there was sometimes so much
asbestos dust in the air

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:12.119
that kids could write their names in it
when it settled.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:16.490
We know all that now, so:
why haven't they changed the name?

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.750
In 2006, the town's then-mayor
tried to change it,

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.119
but the idea was voted down.

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:25.439
And I wanted to find out why, but no-one from
the town wanted to talk to me.

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:29.920
I emailed quite a few places, the local government,
the historical society.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.039
Everyone either said no, they weren't interested,
or just didn't reply.

00:01:34.039 --> 00:01:39.469
They were polite, of course, they're Canadian,
but it wasn't for them.

00:01:39.469 --> 00:01:44.739
And it took me perhaps a little bit too long
to realise why.

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:48.219
The name attracts people like me
and viewers like you.

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:52.139
I knew this video was going to be titled
"A Town Called Asbestos" right away,

00:01:52.139 --> 00:01:54.619
it's the obvious title,
it's got a ring to it.

00:01:54.619 --> 00:01:59.289
Which is why there's already a five-part series
on YouTube from 2011 called exactly that,

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:02.539
put together by a researcher who spent years
with this town.

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:07.319
She turned her PhD into a book, also titled
"A Town Called Asbestos".

00:02:07.319 --> 00:02:10.500
Vice.com published a series
by a German photojournalist,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.939
titled "A Town Called Asbestos".

00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:18.349
All the time, people turn up here, to document
The Town with The Name.

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:21.090
Some of them, like this author,
are thorough and sympathetic

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.269
and take decades of history into account.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:25.530
Some aren't.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.190
Some just set up a camera by the mine overlook,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.610
turn up for a couple of hours and film something.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.810
Others don't even visit, they just make fun
of them from the other side of the world.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.469
"Why? Because the town's name is Asbestos."
[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]

00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:41.069
How do you feel about the name?
Why don't you change it?

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:44.180
They've answered these same questions
for years,

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.400
and I suspect they're
very, very tired of it.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.550
The clearest answer that I've found is from
an interview in the Globe and Mail in 2016.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.870
Ghislain Tessier, vice-president of a local
chamber of commerce, said:

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.780
"Asbestos was our lives.
It was our heritage."

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.900
And I think that's the key to why this town
is still called Asbestos.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.489
Because, yes, for most of the world,
the name is alarming.

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:10.260
But here, that's tempered with the fact that
this mine,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.209
like a coal mine or a gold mine,
it was how people made their living.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:16.250
Lots of people worked in the mine,

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.079
and their parents, and their grandparents,
and their great-grandparents.

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:24.310
It was part of their identity, a mineral that
the world wanted to use,

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.920
it was made right here by them,
and it saved lives,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.360
and it really was something to be proud of.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.010
Asbestos, and this mine, was defended by the
folks who worked here.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:36.409
In '97, just after France banned asbestos,
four men from this town,

00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:38.500
four of the luckier ones,

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.829
ran the Paris Marathon.

00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:42.750
And they were congratulated in a statement
in Canada's Parliament

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.469
for showing that the risks weren't that great.

00:03:45.469 --> 00:03:49.379
Because the risks were drastically downplayed
by management and by government.

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:53.599
Even after the world at large agreed
how dangerous asbestos is,

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:57.840
economics and the desire for profit meant
that it was still mined for decades here.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.980
This mine only closed in 2012.
It's only been a few years.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.480
Maybe the name will change,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.560
if only to stop jerks like me coming along
and going, 'ooh, look at this place'.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.860
But not just yet.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.379
Dr Jessica van Horssen's book is the definitive
history of the town and the mine,

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:17.419
a lot of this video is based on it, and I
thoroughly recommend it.

